\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {3}The Two-Dimensional Turn: Why a Surface, Not a Line}{13}{chapter.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.1}What One Dimension Could and Could Not Ask}{13}{section.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.2}First: Is 2D Testable At All?}{13}{section.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The pre-build feasibility table. Effective decades per coordinate, measured from the 1D survey. The limiting coordinate governs each pair. Hertzsprung--Russell --- the most obvious same-object pair in astronomy --- is dead on the colour axis, which spans 0.3 decades and cannot resolve even register 4. Of every candidate pair, \textbf  {exactly one} (kinematic--luminosity) has both coordinates spanning enough range to test jointly, and even it reaches only mid registers.}}{14}{table.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.3}The Geometry of the Difference}{15}{section.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The dimensional turn. In one dimension the lattice is a comb and the test asks whether a value falls on a tick. In two dimensions the lattice is a grid of nodes on a plane, and the test asks whether a coordinate \emph  {pair} falls on a grid point. The 2D test is strictly richer: a pair can miss the grid even when each coordinate hits its own comb, and vice versa.}}{15}{figure.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.4}The Lock Statistic in Two Dimensions}{15}{section.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.5}What Compounds, and Must Be Handled Per-Coordinate}{15}{section.3.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.6}Why the Scramble Null Finally Works}{16}{section.3.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Why Erratum 1 becomes a tool. Shuffling one coordinate against the other preserves both marginal distributions --- the same values are present on each axis --- but destroys the pairing. If the real pairs lie on a lattice and the scrambled pairs do not, the difference is genuine joint structure. The scramble null, meaningless in 1D, is the central falsifier in 2D.}}{16}{figure.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.7}The Engine Pair, Mirrored From 1D}{17}{section.3.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The 2D engine pair mirrors the 1D pair, with the scramble null now load-bearing in both.}}{17}{table.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3.8}What Comes Next}{18}{section.3.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {4}First Light: Building the 2D Period Engine and Proving It Honest}{20}{chapter.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.1}The Design Decision That Shaped Everything}{20}{section.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.2}The Statistic, Lifted to a Surface}{21}{section.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.3}The Gates, Designed In}{21}{section.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.3.1}The scramble null --- why two dimensions redeems it}{21}{subsection.4.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.3.2}The look-elsewhere correction, found by the synthetic lakes}{21}{subsection.4.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.4}The Synthetic Lakes: Controls With Known Answers}{22}{section.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The four 2D control lakes. Three must come back null; one must survive. A panel that only ever passes proves the instrument agrees with itself, not that it can disagree --- so the single-axis control is a pre-registered \emph  {expected fail}, planted to look like a signal and required to be rejected.}}{22}{table.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.5}First Light}{22}{section.4.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The verification instrument, not an illustration. Each control lake's joint density is shown as it is (REAL) and with one coordinate shuffled against the other (SCRAMBLED). A diagonal correlation ridge survives the shuffle; a true joint lattice lock vanishes under it. The eye catches in the heatmap what a table of 529 pair-scores hides --- which is why visual inspection is part of the verification chain from here, not a presentation step.}}{23}{figure.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4.6}What First Light Establishes}{23}{section.4.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {5}First Light: The 2D Phase Engine and Why It Needs the Scramble}{25}{chapter.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.1}A Second Instrument, a Different Question}{25}{section.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.2}The Two Nulls, and Why Both Are Needed}{25}{section.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.3}First Light}{26}{section.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces 2D phase engine first light. The single-axis row is the result that matters: the chaos null reports a $48\sigma $ ``signal,'' and the scramble null correctly reduces it to nothing. A chaos-only engine would have announced a false discovery; the scramble refuses it.}}{26}{table.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Phase engine first light, best-of-grid ratios as returned. The one lake with a planted joint lock clears the ratio bar at exactly the registers where it was buried; the three that must not survive --- pure noise, a correlation ridge, and a single-axis lock --- all sit within a hair of unity. Compare the period engine's own first-light pair, which reproduced the same structure at a far larger ratio: \texttt  {synth\_jointlock} at $16.91$, \texttt  {synth\_noise} at $1.09$. The two statistics agree on which lakes lock and disagree only on how loudly, exactly as their different dynamic ranges predict.}}{27}{table.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces The phase engine's verification heatmap. For each control lake the joint node density is shown as measured (REAL) and with one coordinate shuffled (SCRAMBLED). The single-axis lake is the instructive one: its real panel shows a band of node structure on one axis, and that band \emph  {survives} the shuffle --- visibly marking it as single-axis, not joint. The true joint lock, by contrast, is a grid of cells in the real panel that dissolves under the shuffle. The eye reads in the panel what the chaos $z$-score alone would have misreported.}}{28}{figure.5.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.4}The Form A Gate Remains Open}{29}{section.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5.5}Two Engines, Both Honest}{29}{section.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The matched 2D engine pair. Both found their planted locks at the correct registers, rejected noise, killed a Form B correlation, and refused a single-axis artifact. The period engine achieves a larger dynamic range because its statistic carries no phase origin; the phase engine achieves honesty on the single-axis case only through the scramble null.}}{29}{table.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {6}First Real Data: Velocity $\times $ Luminosity, and the Null We Called}{30}{chapter.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.1}The One Pair the Feasibility Table Admitted}{30}{section.6.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.2}The Build}{30}{section.6.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Promotion pre-flight, as returned. The resolution gate is $M/N > 2.67$. Luminosity clears it comfortably at register $15$; velocity does not. The build printed its own warning: \emph  {``one or both coordinates fail the $M/N > 2.67$ resolution gate at $15/\pi $.''} The gate measured the data and told the truth about it before a single pair was scored.}}{31}{table.6.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.3}What the Support Audit Showed}{31}{section.6.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Support audit of the promoted lake, $n = 83{,}155$. Luminosity carries 5.3 effective decades; velocity carries only 1.9. The joint correlation is a faint $0.15$ --- real, but weak.}}{31}{table.6.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces The resolution boundary, drawn to scale. Luminosity (5.3 decades) reaches to about register 14; velocity (1.9 decades) runs out of range just above register 8. Because the joint test is limited by the stricter axis, the pair can test the low-to-mid registers and is blind above them. The dashed line marks the pre-build velocity estimate (3.3 decades) that the realized lake undershot --- the doorway was narrower than projected.}}{32}{figure.6.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.4}The Verdict: Both Engines, Noise Floor}{32}{section.6.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Why the null is a true null. For a planted joint lock, the scramble dissolves a visible grid of cells (Chapters 5--6). Here, the real and scrambled panels are statistically indistinguishable --- one diffuse blob either way --- because the pairing carried no joint lattice information to begin with. There was no structure to destroy, which is exactly what ratio $0.88$ means.}}{33}{figure.6.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.5}The Prediction, Confirmed}{33}{section.6.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.6}Why This Null Is Trustworthy: The Controls Passed Alongside}{33}{section.6.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces The real lake and its controls, scored in one run. The instrument demonstrably locks when a lock is present (\texttt  {synth\_jointlock}) and stays quiet when it is not. The velocity$\times $luminosity null therefore is a \emph  {measurement}, not a dead pipeline --- the engine that returned it had just proven, in the same breath, that it can return the opposite.}}{33}{table.6.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.7}The Caveat We Will Not Bury}{34}{section.6.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6.8}What Would Change the Answer}{34}{section.6.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {7}The Door Closes: The Nuclear Dual-Wide Probe}{36}{chapter.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.1}The Last Candidate}{36}{section.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.2}The Probe: a Pull, Not a Run}{36}{section.7.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.3}The Result}{37}{section.7.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The nuclear door, closed to scale. Half-life towers nearly three times over the resolution gate; decay energy sits at roughly a sixth of it. The two-dimensional test requires both bars above the dashed line. One is, one is not --- and the joint test is governed by the lower bar, so the pair cannot resolve even the first register honestly. This is a go/no-go measurement, not a score: no register was tested, only whether a test was possible.}}{37}{figure.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.4}The Same Lesson, Twice}{38}{section.7.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.5}The Two-Dimensional Survey Is Closed}{38}{section.7.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The exhaustive two-dimensional feasibility census. Of every same-object pair in the survey, none carries two coordinates each clearing the resolution gate on a populated join. The one pair that resolved a single axis to mid registers (velocity$\times $luminosity) was built and returned a bounded null; the one remaining candidate (half-life$\times $energy) was probed and collapsed on its second axis.}}{38}{table.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7.6}What This Closes, and What It Opens}{39}{section.7.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{Preface: Why a Compass}{40}{chapter*.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {chapter}{\numberline {8}The End of the Ladder, and the Turn to Direction}{42}{chapter.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{lot}{\addvspace {10\p@ }}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8.1}Where the Scalar Ladder Stops}{42}{section.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces The dimensional ladder. Each scalar rung demands one more wide axis on the same objects, and each is harder to feed than the last: the 1D survey is nearly exhausted, the 2D survey is empty on current data (Volume 14), and a 3D scalar engine would need three wide same-object axes that do not exist. The vector turn is not a further rung --- it is a step sideways, onto a question with abundant data of its own.}}{42}{figure.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8.2}What ``Vector'' Adds That ``Scalar'' Cannot Hold}{43}{section.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces A scalar carries one fact: its size. A vector carries two, which separate cleanly --- a magnitude $|\mathbf  {v}|$ (a scalar, and the only thing the old engines could ever see) and a direction $\hat  {\mathbf  {v}} = \mathbf  {v}/|\mathbf  {v}|$ (a point on a sphere, which no engine has yet tested).}}{43}{figure.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
